Calc: inserting or deleting rows no longer searches every broadcast slot for listeners that do not exist.

A Linux user reported that inserting a row in Sheets takes about 10 seconds. Workbooks where every row
looks something up in whole columns (VLOOKUP(D5;DanhMuc!$A:$D;2;0), SUMIF($D:$D;D5;$I:$I), COUNTIF)
reproduce it: on a synthetic 20 000-row ledger with such formulas, conditional formats, validation,
a filter, a table and a chart, one Insert Rows took 7-13 s and one Delete Rows 8-17 s (1.0.2 and the
26.8.3.1 base; 1.0.1: 5-10 s), and the VLOOKUP columns alone 2-5 s.

perf on the 26.8.3.1 base: 60-68 % of the time is std::unordered_set::find under
ScBroadcastAreaSlot::EndListeningArea <- ScBroadcastAreaSlotMachine::EndListeningArea <-
ScFormulaCell::EndListeningTo <- ScColumn::UpdateReference <- ScDocument::InsertRow/DeleteRow.
Formula groups made by the import listen to their range references as a group
(SharedFormulaUtil::startListeningAsGroup, group area listener). When rows are inserted or deleted
above a group, every cell of the group ends its listening one by one and, for each range reference,
also asks the slot machine to end an individual listening of the cell that was never started. The slot
machine then looked for that area in every slot the range covers: a whole column spans 896 slots
(256 + 5 x 128 row slices up to 1 048 576 rows), so 20 000 cells x 3 lookups x 896 slots per insert.

An area is always inserted into the slot of its range start first, and StartListeningArea() looks for
an existing area only there. So when that first slot does not have the area, no slot has it, and
when the area still has listeners after this listener left, the other slots have nothing to do (they
only drop an area that has no listeners left). EndListeningArea now stops after the first slot in
both cases; an area that lost its last listener is still removed from every slot as before.

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).
Test: CppunitTest_sc_ucalc_sharedformula testSharedFormulaWholeColumnShiftEndListening (insert and
delete rows above 20 000 grouped whole-column lookups vs. the same lookups into a bounded range, and
the results still follow the catalogue afterwards); tests/runtime/check-sheets-insert-row-speed.py.
